pillbox+recWG+load.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pillbox+recWG+load.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic:  1, 2
    Exterior:  6
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 1
    Epsilon:   1.0
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 2
    Epsilon:   10.0
    EpsilonImag: -1.52         // loss tangent 0.152
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           1
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 2
  FrequencyShift:  1.1e9
}
</code></pre> <p><code>EpsilonImag</code> is negative for a lossy material. There is no <code>Waveguide</code> boundary and no <code>Port</code> block; the loss comes entirely from the volume. The problem is still a complex eigenproblem.</p>

dlwg-pbc4-m0.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pbc-4.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Exterior: 6
    Magnetic: 1, 2
    Periodic_M: 3              // master face
    Periodic_S: 4              // slave face
    Theta: -150                // phase advance per cell, degrees
  }
  SurfaceMaterial: {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 1
  FrequencyShift:  1.10e9
}
</code></pre> <p><code>Periodic_M</code> and <code>Periodic_S</code> name the master and slave sidesets. <code>Theta</code> is the phase advance in degrees. The sign convention follows the direction from master to slave. For a \(2\pi/3\) structure, set <code>Theta</code> to 120 or -120 and compare the frequency against the design.</p>

pillbox-rtop.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pillbox-rtop4.ncdf

  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic: 1, 2          // symmetry planes: monopole modes
    Exterior: 6             // cavity wall
  }

  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7            // copper, S/m, for Q0
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 2
  FrequencyShift:  1.0e9    // look for modes above 1 GHz
}

PostProcess : {
  Toggle: on                // write field files for ParaView
  ModeFile: mode
}
</code></pre> <p>Two magnetic symmetry planes select modes whose electric field is normal to both planes, which for a cylindrical cavity means the monopole family. To get dipole modes, change one plane to <code>Electric</code>.</p> <p>The default volume material is vacuum, so no <code>Material</code> block is needed. If you want to be explicit:</p> <pre><code>  Material : {
    Attribute: 1            // Cubit block ID
    Epsilon: 1.0
    Mu: 1.0
  }
</code></pre>

pillbox-rtop+coax.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pillbox-rtop+coax2.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic:  2
    Electric:  3, 4          // beam pipe ends, below cutoff
    Exterior:  6
    Waveguide: 7             // coax port
  }
  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
  Tolerance: 1e-7
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 1
  FrequencyShift:  1.0e9
}

Port : {
  ReferenceNumber : 7
  NumberOfModes : 1          // the coax TEM mode
}

PostProcess : {
  Toggle: on
  ModeFile: mode
}
</code></pre> <p>The beam pipe ends are closed with <code>Electric</code>. That is fine here because the pipe is below cutoff at 1.3 GHz, so the field there has decayed to nothing. The coax port is <code>Waveguide</code> with one mode loaded: a coax has a TEM mode with zero cutoff, and nothing else propagates at this frequency.</p>

90DegreeBend.s3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: ./90DegreeBend.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Exterior: 6
    Waveguide: 7, 8
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

FrequencyScan: {
  Start:    9.424e+9
  End:      12.424e+9
  Interval: 0.25e+9
}

PostProcess: {
  Toggle: on
  PortNumber: 7
}

Port: {
  ReferenceNumber: 7
  NumberOfModes: 2
}
Port: {
  ReferenceNumber: 8
  NumberOfModes: 2
}
</code></pre> <p>No <code>SurfaceMaterial</code> block is given, so the walls are treated as lossless. Two modes are loaded per port. Only TE<sub>10</sub> propagates below 13.1 GHz, so the second mode is evanescent across the scan and is included to absorb near-port field distortion.</p>

recWG-load1.s3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: ./recWG-load1.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Magnetic: 1
    Exterior: 6
    Waveguide: 7
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 1
    Epsilon:   1.0
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 2
    Epsilon:   10.0
    Mu:        1.0
    EpsilonImag: -1.52
  }
  SurfaceMaterial: {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

FrequencyScan: {
  Start:    2.556e+9
  End:      3.156e+9
  Interval: 0.05e+9
}

PostProcess: {
  Toggle: on
  PortNumber: 7
}

Port: {
  ReferenceNumber: 7
  NumberOfModes: 2
}
</code></pre> <p>The material blocks are the same as in the Omega3P lossy load tutorial. Block attribute 2 is the wedge, with a negative imaginary permittivity for loss.</p>

window.s3p<pre><code>ModelInfo: {
  File: ./window.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition: {
    Magnetic: 1
    Electric: 2
    Exterior: 6
    Waveguide: 7, 8
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 1
    Epsilon:   1.0
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  Material : {
    Attribute: 2
    Epsilon:   9.37
    Mu:        1.0
  }
  SurfaceMaterial: {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order: 2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

FrequencyScan: {
  Start:    2.356e9
  End:      3.856e9
  Interval: 0.1e+9
}
</code></pre> <p>The quarter model uses one magnetic and one electric symmetry plane, which is the correct pair for the TE<sub>10</sub> mode of the rectangular guide. Without <code>Port</code> blocks, S3P loads one mode per waveguide surface, which is right for a single-moded guide.</p>

Pillbox.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./Pillbox.ncdf
  BoundaryCondition : {
    Exterior: 1, 2, 6
  }
  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}
FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}
EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 2
  FrequencyShift:  1.0e9
}
</code></pre> <p>Sidesets 1 and 2 are the beam pipe ends and 6 is the wall. All three are <code>Exterior</code> here because this is a full model with no symmetry planes. Omega3P writes <code>omega3p_results/omega3p.l0.m0000.1.3138e+09.mod</code>, the TM<sub>010</sub> mode that Track3P will load.</p> <p>Mode file is required</p> <p>Track3P reads the <code>.mod</code> field file, so the <code>PostProcess</code> block in the Omega3P input must be on, or omitted so that the default applies. If Omega3P was run with <code>PostProcess: { Toggle: off }</code>, rerun it.</p>

Pillbox.track3p<pre><code>FieldScales: {
  Type: FieldGradient
  ScanToken: 1
  Minimum: 23.0e+6
  Maximum: 25.0e+6
  Interval: 1.0e+6
}

NormalizedField: {
  StartPoint: 0.0 0.0 -0.057
  EndPoint:   0.0 0.0  0.057
}

Domain: {
  FieldDir: ./omega3p_results
}

Emitter: {
  BoundaryID: 6
  x0: 0.03   x1: 0.035
  y0: 0.04   y1: 0.1
  z0: -0.045 z1: 0.0
}

Material: { Type: Primary    BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Secondary  BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Absorber   BoundarySurfaceID: 1 2 }

OutputImpacts: on

Postprocess: {
  Toggle: on
  ResonantParticles: { Token: on }
  EnhancementCounter: {
    Token: on
    MinimumEC: 0.01
    SEYFileName1: copper.dat
    BoundarySurfaceID1: 6
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>The emitter box restricts launch sites to a patch of the cavity wall near the equator, which keeps the run short. The normalization line spans the 90 mm cavity length, plus a little, so that the scan levels are accelerating gradients in the usual sense. The beam pipe ends absorb.</p> <p>Run it with 16 threads on one rank; the three levels finish in about two minutes:</p> <pre><code>export OMP_NUM_THREADS=16
srun -n 1 -c 16 track3p Pillbox.track3p
</code></pre> <p>The end of <code>track3p.log</code> reports the scale factor applied to the normalized Omega3P field at each level:</p> <pre><code>number of all emitting faces = 16
scale 1.144609569160e+06 Field 2.300000000000e+07
scale 1.194375202602e+06 Field 2.400000000000e+07
scale 1.244140836043e+06 Field 2.500000000000e+07
Done!
</code></pre>

Tesla-PEmission.track3p<pre><code>ParticlesTrajectories: {
  Skip: 2                    // save every other step
}
TotalTime: 6

FieldScales: {
  Type: FieldGradient
  ScanToken: 0
  Scale: 16.00e+6            // 16 MV/m
}

NormalizedField: {
  StartPoint: 0.0 0.0 0.0
  EndPoint:   0.0 0.0 1.036200   // full 9-cell length
}

Domain: {
  FieldDir: ./omega3p_results
  Bins: 640                  // long structure: finer binning
  MaxImpacts: 2              // drop particles after two impacts
}

Emitter: {
  Type: 7                    // FieldEmission
  BoundaryID: 6
  t0: 0
  t1: 1
  x0: 0.0   x1: 0.104
  y0: 0.0   y1: 0.104
  z0: 0.0   z1: 0.114        // first cell only
  N: 1000
  WorkFunction: 4.2
  Beta: 120
}

Material: { Type: Primary    BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Secondary  BoundarySurfaceID: 6 }
Material: { Type: Absorber   BoundarySurfaceID: 1 2 }

OutputImpacts: on
</code></pre> <p><code>Bins: 640</code> matters here. The default of 64 is tuned for a compact cavity; a one-meter structure with fine irises needs many more bins for particle localization to be efficient. <code>MaxImpacts: 2</code> keeps the run to primaries and their first bounce, since secondaries are switched off by the absence of a <code>Model: 2</code> secondary material.</p> <p>Run with the results directory named explicitly:</p> <pre><code>srun -n 16 track3p Tesla-PEmission.track3p track3p_PEmission
</code></pre>

pillbox.omega3p<pre><code>ModelInfo : {
  File: ./pillbox4.ncdf

  BoundaryCondition : {
    Magnetic: 1, 2
    Exterior: 6
  }

  SurfaceMaterial : {
    ReferenceNumber: 6
    Sigma: 5.8e7
  }
}

FiniteElement: {
  Order:           2
  CurvedSurfaces: on
}

EigenSolver : {
  NumEigenvalues: 2
  FrequencyShift:  1.0e9
}

PostProcess : {
  Toggle: on
  ModeFile: mode
}
</code></pre> <p>Rules:</p> <ul> <li>A block is <code>Name : { ... }</code>. Blocks nest. The space before the colon is optional.</li> <li>A scalar entry is <code>Key: value</code>. Lists are comma separated: <code>Magnetic: 1, 2</code>.</li> <li>Comments use <code>//</code> to end of line or <code>/* ... */</code> for a range. Commenting out a whole block is the usual way to disable it.</li> <li>Keywords are case sensitive. <code>Order</code> and <code>order</code> are different.</li> <li>A block may be repeated when the solver expects a list of them, for example several <code>Material</code> or <code>Port</code> blocks.</li> <li>Numbers accept scientific notation: <code>5.8e7</code>, <code>1.0e9</code>.</li> <li>Switches accept <code>on</code> and <code>off</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Keyword reference</p> <p>The reference section lists every keyword each solver reads, with its type and default value, generated from the source code. Use it to check spelling and defaults.</p>
